Subject: Re: Maximum memory allocation to Java
In-Reply-To: <20110118185542.GA10980@sfu.ca>
References: <20110118185542.GA10980@sfu.ca>
Message-ID: <F85248CC-03ED-4A88-B8BC-512F70D62AA2@r-project.org>


On Jan 18, 2011, at 1:55 PM, Sigal Blay wrote:

> In order to test what is the maximum memory that can be allocated 
> to Java from R we're using:
> system("java -Xms512m -Xmx1536m")  
> (trying different values for the second parameter).
> 
> On my Windows machine,
> system("java -Xms512m -Xmx1536m") works, but setting java.parameter to 1536MB 
> before issuing .jpackage(package, lib.loc = libname) in zzz.R
> fail package installation with "Could not reserve enough space for object heap". 
> It works when I set it as low as 1394MB.
> Looks like .jpackage uses some overhead.

Not at all, but you have to remember that the memory available depends on all programs running on your machine which does include R. Also your package could be loaded at any point (e.g., after the user has already used half of the memory on your machine) so defining any fixed threshold is in general not useful at all.


> This is not the case on the Linux machine I'm working on.
> 
> The question is, is there a fixed value to the memory overhead on different  machines? Is there a way I can determine it, or a range I can expect?
> 

There is no "overhead" in that sense. The memory available to Java depends on the memory available on the machine in general as a given point in time and that is way different even on the same machine. It also varies strongly by OS because some OSes are capable of supplying more mapped memory under different conditions (32-bit Windows has in particular very low limits and capabilities). It is best left to the user to rise the limits as needed (unfortunately Java is not capable of using a flexible limits AFAIR which is the actual problem).

Cheers,
Simon

